WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:02.402
Let's imagine a modest-sized 
skyscraper...

00:00:02.602 --> 00:00:04.971
about 100 meters and 
30 stories tall.

00:00:05.105 --> 00:00:07.374
A high rise this size was 
almost certainly built

00:00:07.374 --> 00:00:10.143
with concrete, which 
contains cement.

00:00:10.143 --> 00:00:12.779
In this case, about 
6,000 tons of cement.

00:00:13.213 --> 00:00:16.916
Making that cement probably 
emitted about 4,600 metric tons

00:00:16.916 --> 00:00:18.485
of carbon into the atmosphere.

00:00:18.718 --> 00:00:22.889
That's about the emissions
from driving a car for 12 million miles.

00:00:23.023 --> 00:00:25.959
Now, multiply that by all 
the buildings in the world.

00:00:26.393 --> 00:00:29.162
Even that wouldn't capture 
cement's carbon footprint.

00:00:29.362 --> 00:00:31.831
Think about the sidewalks
we’re walking on.

00:00:31.831 --> 00:00:33.666
Many of the streets you drive on.

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:35.468
A lot of our energy infrastructure.

00:00:35.468 --> 00:00:37.170
Dams, power plants.

00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:39.706
All that cement production 
accounts for 8%

00:00:39.706 --> 00:00:41.574
of all global carbon emissions...

00:00:41.775 --> 00:00:45.045
more than the aviation and 
the shipping industries combined.

00:00:45.111 --> 00:00:46.613
Today, most of those emissions

00:00:46.613 --> 00:00:48.648
are due to China's rapid development.

00:00:48.748 --> 00:00:52.052
In just two years, China produced
more cement than the US did

00:00:52.052 --> 00:00:54.020
in the entire 20th century.

00:00:54.654 --> 00:00:56.923
Other developing countries 
will use a lot of cement

00:00:56.923 --> 00:00:59.492
as they build tall cities 
and infrastructure too.

00:01:00.326 --> 00:01:03.063
If we want to reach net zero 
in a few decades

00:01:03.063 --> 00:01:05.298
we'll have to figure out how to build 
a building like this...

00:01:05.665 --> 00:01:07.300
without emissions.

00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:09.069
It'll be hard, but there's a way.

00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:12.672
You're looking at 
a typical cement plant.

00:01:12.672 --> 00:01:14.374
One like this is currently operating

00:01:14.374 --> 00:01:16.509
in nearly every region of the world.

00:01:16.509 --> 00:01:20.547
This rotating tube, a kiln, and 
this tower, a pre heater

00:01:20.547 --> 00:01:22.749
are where all cement's emissions take place.

00:01:22.949 --> 00:01:25.118
About 40% of cement's emissions

00:01:25.118 --> 00:01:27.720
are a result of burning fuel 
to heat the kiln.

00:01:27.921 --> 00:01:31.724
It reaches around 1450 degrees Celsius 
near the heat source.

00:01:31.958 --> 00:01:34.727
The preheating tower is where 
limestone, clays

00:01:34.727 --> 00:01:36.463
and other additives are dropped.

00:01:36.463 --> 00:01:38.965
The 850 degree temperature here

00:01:38.965 --> 00:01:42.235
causes limestone to release 
its stored carbon dioxide

00:01:42.268 --> 00:01:44.971
and those are the other 
60% of emissions.

00:01:45.672 --> 00:01:47.874
The first question about 
decarbonizing cement

00:01:47.874 --> 00:01:51.211
is whether we can just use 
less concrete altogether.

00:01:51.211 --> 00:01:55.081
We typically use up to 2 to 3 times
too much concrete in design.

00:01:55.081 --> 00:01:58.184
Architects and structural engineers 
just use lots of it.

00:01:58.218 --> 00:02:01.054
It doesn't hit the bottom line and 
it makes the thing more reliable

00:02:01.054 --> 00:02:03.223
and they're less likely 
to get sued and kill people 

00:02:03.223 --> 00:02:04.190
and what have you.

00:02:04.190 --> 00:02:07.794
In a building, concrete is valuable 
for its compressive strength:

00:02:07.794 --> 00:02:09.963
its ability to hold up under
a lot of weight.

00:02:10.063 --> 00:02:12.432
So it's hard to substitute 
its use in places like 

00:02:12.432 --> 00:02:14.367
foundations and columns.

00:02:14.367 --> 00:02:16.136
But elsewhere, we should minimize it.

00:02:16.169 --> 00:02:18.905
I was in the new Parliament building 
in Scotland

00:02:18.905 --> 00:02:21.941
and it was obviously designed with 
greenhouse gases in mind.

00:02:21.941 --> 00:02:24.144
Concrete was only used where needed.

00:02:24.144 --> 00:02:26.146
Steel was only used where needed.

00:02:26.146 --> 00:02:28.481
And they used laminated wood for the roof

00:02:28.481 --> 00:02:29.816
for a lot of structural materials

00:02:29.816 --> 00:02:31.484
things that weren't load bearing.

00:02:31.484 --> 00:02:33.953
Carbon conscious design 
can certainly chip away

00:02:33.953 --> 00:02:35.088
at cement's emissions.

00:02:35.088 --> 00:02:36.990
But replacing concrete altogether 

00:02:36.990 --> 00:02:38.758
won't be possible in the 
near term.

00:02:38.791 --> 00:02:41.427
So cutting down on excess concrete 
can cut emissions

00:02:41.427 --> 00:02:45.031
on our high rise by roughly 26%, 
according to one analysis.

00:02:45.298 --> 00:02:46.399
We'll have to do more

00:02:46.399 --> 00:02:48.968
and the next place to start 
is how cement is made.

00:02:48.968 --> 00:02:51.237
Looking at the 40% of emissions

00:02:51.237 --> 00:02:54.007
that go into firing the kiln 
is one place to start.

00:02:54.107 --> 00:02:57.076
Typically, cement plants use 
coal or petroleum coke

00:02:57.076 --> 00:03:00.880
or natural gas to heat the kiln 
to 1450 degrees Celsius.

00:03:00.947 --> 00:03:02.382
You could electrify that

00:03:02.382 --> 00:03:03.816
but it's really hard to achieve

00:03:03.816 --> 00:03:05.852
that high of a temperature 
with electric heat.

00:03:05.852 --> 00:03:08.821
Though some startup companies 
like this Finnish one are trying.

00:03:09.122 --> 00:03:10.156
Until we get there

00:03:10.156 --> 00:03:12.759
cement plants have started to just 
burn different things.

00:03:12.992 --> 00:03:15.595
The high heat makes cement plants 
a good place to incinerate

00:03:15.595 --> 00:03:18.331
industrial waste, trash,
or used tires.

00:03:18.364 --> 00:03:21.901
The limestone basically 
scrubs out any nasties

00:03:21.901 --> 00:03:24.604
that are produced in the flame here...

00:03:24.837 --> 00:03:28.741
and stops them being emitted 
into the atmosphere.

00:03:28.741 --> 00:03:31.778
Switching fuels can cut emissions 
by roughly 7%.

00:03:31.945 --> 00:03:33.613
We've still got a ways to go

00:03:33.613 --> 00:03:36.785
so let's look at the other way
cement causes emissions:

00:03:36.785 --> 00:03:39.107
The chemical process.

00:03:39.107 --> 00:03:42.045
This is the carbon released from 
the heated limestone.

00:03:42.045 --> 00:03:43.232
Once it goes through the kiln

00:03:43.232 --> 00:03:45.612
it becomes a material 
called clinker:

00:03:45.612 --> 00:03:47.937
the key binding ingredient in cement.

00:03:47.937 --> 00:03:52.314
And cement binds together the rocks 
and sand and water in concrete.

00:03:52.314 --> 00:03:54.554
Cement is about 10% of concrete

00:03:54.554 --> 00:03:56.809
but accounts for a majority 
of its emissions.

00:03:56.809 --> 00:04:00.501
So one strategy is to find 
a substitute for the clinker.

00:04:00.501 --> 00:04:01.928
Startups have been in a race

00:04:01.928 --> 00:04:03.905
to develop a new kind 
of green cement

00:04:03.905 --> 00:04:05.573
that avoids clinker altogether.

00:04:05.573 --> 00:04:07.901
But so far, nothing is as abundant

00:04:07.901 --> 00:04:10.629
and commercially viable as 
the limestone-based stuff.

00:04:10.629 --> 00:04:12.284
And adjusting concrete formulas

00:04:12.284 --> 00:04:14.287
takes a lot of time and safety testing.

00:04:14.287 --> 00:04:15.527
For good reason.

00:04:15.527 --> 00:04:18.031
The events in Turkey recently,

00:04:18.031 --> 00:04:21.100
there was a lot of substandard 
cement being used there

00:04:21.100 --> 00:04:24.169
and there was a lot of substandard 
building practices.

00:04:24.169 --> 00:04:25.259
In North America

00:04:25.259 --> 00:04:27.343
clinker accounts for about 
90% of cement

00:04:27.343 --> 00:04:28.685
due to safety standards.

00:04:28.685 --> 00:04:30.690
But North America 
is overly cautious

00:04:30.690 --> 00:04:32.400
compared to the rest of the world.

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:34.577
The average clinker-to-cement ratio globally

00:04:34.577 --> 00:04:37.145
was about 72% in 2020.

00:04:37.145 --> 00:04:39.900
That's made possible by 
clinker-like substitutes.

00:04:39.900 --> 00:04:42.332
We could reduce the clinker ratio even more.

00:04:42.332 --> 00:04:45.000
Experts emphasized one new 
cement mixture

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:47.704
that gets the clinker ratio 
safely down to 50%

00:04:47.704 --> 00:04:51.107
by supplementing it with more clay 
and unprocessed limestone.

00:04:51.107 --> 00:04:54.461
So that’s with an existing technology
that meets existing building codes.

00:04:54.461 --> 00:04:55.719
That'll cut half the emissions

00:04:55.719 --> 00:04:57.501
from the cement and concrete industry.

00:04:57.501 --> 00:05:00.000
These reductions will help 
in reducing emissions...

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:03.149
but until we find a scalable, 
zero-emission cement,

00:05:03.149 --> 00:05:04.843
any clinker production at all

00:05:04.843 --> 00:05:07.382
will inevitably have process emissions.

00:05:07.382 --> 00:05:10.196
Which brings us to the last piece 
of decarbonization.

00:05:10.196 --> 00:05:12.306
The cement industry will need to use

00:05:12.306 --> 00:05:14.242
carbon capture and storage 
to get there.

00:05:14.242 --> 00:05:15.506
There's no way around it.

00:05:15.506 --> 00:05:17.704
This would mean capturing the carbon emitted

00:05:17.704 --> 00:05:20.198
from the heat and chemical processes here...

00:05:20.198 --> 00:05:23.751
and storing it deep underground 
in a geologic deposit.

00:05:23.751 --> 00:05:25.513
A cement company in Norway is piloting

00:05:25.513 --> 00:05:28.940
one of the first cement plants in the world 
to capture carbon.

00:05:28.940 --> 00:05:30.747
And they'll store it under the North Sea

00:05:30.747 --> 00:05:32.855
in their oil and gas deposits.

00:05:32.855 --> 00:05:35.562
Some new companies are also finding a way to inject

00:05:35.562 --> 00:05:38.955
stored carbon back into cement and concrete 
during production.

00:05:38.955 --> 00:05:42.603
This takes advantage of rock's 
natural ability to reabsorb carbon.

00:05:42.603 --> 00:05:46.746
And potentially one day we may even see 
our built environment

00:05:46.746 --> 00:05:48.004
become a carbon sink

00:05:48.004 --> 00:05:51.479
which we don't usually think about 
when we think about large

00:05:51.479 --> 00:05:54.193
concrete cities and large buildings

00:05:54.193 --> 00:05:56.156
and our roads and all of that.

00:05:56.156 --> 00:05:57.544
So this is the scenario

00:05:57.544 --> 00:06:00.124
in which we get this building 
to net zero emissions.

00:06:00.124 --> 00:06:02.816
And the goal right now 
is to get there by 2050.

00:06:02.816 --> 00:06:05.751
That requires us to start now 
and aggressively.

00:06:05.751 --> 00:06:08.855
Especially in China, the world's 
biggest producer.

00:06:08.855 --> 00:06:10.906
But experts told me there's 
some good news there.

00:06:10.906 --> 00:06:14.221
So in the US, the average age 
of a cement plant

00:06:14.221 --> 00:06:15.930
is about 34, 35 years.

00:06:15.930 --> 00:06:19.099
In China, it's 15, which means 
that many of their plants

00:06:19.099 --> 00:06:20.918
are actually more energy efficient

00:06:20.918 --> 00:06:23.011
than the plants that we have here.

00:06:23.011 --> 00:06:26.165
China uses less clinker in their cement
than the global average

00:06:26.165 --> 00:06:29.124
and they have at least one
carbon capture project in the works.

00:06:29.124 --> 00:06:30.622
But there's a long way to go.

00:06:30.622 --> 00:06:31.890
Last year, they pushed back

00:06:31.890 --> 00:06:36.326
their peak building emissions deadline 
from 2025 to 2030.

00:06:36.326 --> 00:06:39.045
Changes to design in the concrete
and cement processes

00:06:39.045 --> 00:06:40.824
will be the easiest to implement...

00:06:40.824 --> 00:06:44.139
but carbon capture and storage 
is at very early stages.

00:06:44.139 --> 00:06:46.063
And all this will be expensive.

00:06:46.063 --> 00:06:48.736
It's estimated that the cement 
used in this building

00:06:48.736 --> 00:06:52.458
will cost somewhere between 
70 to 115% more.

00:06:52.458 --> 00:06:54.751
The cost of cement or concrete

00:06:54.751 --> 00:06:58.334
is a very small fraction of 
the overall cost of a project.

00:06:58.334 --> 00:07:00.648
So it's relatively easy

00:07:00.648 --> 00:07:02.980
and cost effective for 
a government agency

00:07:02.980 --> 00:07:04.645
to commit to pay

00:07:04.645 --> 00:07:07.815
a little more for the material 
to absorb that green premium.

00:07:07.815 --> 00:07:09.807
The US Inflation Reduction Act

00:07:09.807 --> 00:07:12.371
is helping create a market 
for carbon capture and storage

00:07:12.371 --> 00:07:17.834
by increasing tax credits to 
$85 per metric ton of carbon stored.

00:07:17.834 --> 00:07:20.000
For a long time, heavy industries 
like cement

00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:22.474
seemed like unsolvable climate problems.

00:07:22.474 --> 00:07:24.524
But today, we know how to fix them

00:07:24.524 --> 00:07:27.418
and we can't afford not to try.

00:07:27.815 --> 00:07:29.516
Thanks for watching the video.

00:07:29.516 --> 00:07:30.932
As a follow up to this one,

00:07:30.932 --> 00:07:33.242
I'd recommend checking out 
my coworker, Phil Edwards’s

00:07:33.260 --> 00:07:36.363
great video on the world's tallest 
mass timber building

00:07:36.363 --> 00:07:37.932
in Milwaukee, Wisconsin.

00:07:37.958 --> 00:07:40.527
He was able to film the building 
under construction

00:07:40.546 --> 00:07:43.315
and he gives a great explanation 
about how changing

00:07:43.315 --> 00:07:44.598
from traditional materials

00:07:44.598 --> 00:07:46.299
like steel and concrete

00:07:46.299 --> 00:07:49.136
to wood changes the entire 
building process.

00:07:49.136 --> 00:07:50.794
Wood probably can't replace

00:07:50.804 --> 00:07:52.673
concrete in all the world's buildings.

00:07:52.692 --> 00:07:55.861
One expert I interviewed told me 
we'd probably have to triple

00:07:55.861 --> 00:07:57.380
the amount of wood harvested

00:07:57.380 --> 00:07:58.889
in order to scale it up 
and do that

00:07:58.889 --> 00:08:01.529
and that would come with its own 
environmental problems.

00:08:01.529 --> 00:08:03.424
But our big concrete problem requires

00:08:03.424 --> 00:08:05.026
a lot of different solutions

00:08:05.026 --> 00:08:08.036
and using wood when possible 
is definitely one of them.

00:08:08.080 --> 00:08:11.483
And as you can see from Phil's video, 
it looks really cool, too.

